umdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;c0oAVKg8z7W3nxVkpynz8UG11jEFL5JWcKKKsdjaoPkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-23T22:08:03.088Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;1llL5taeeEZ8ar9SV6C7WJ5j6FvAZkF_Jc40iXSB_vQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx„https://ictnews.org/uncategorized/new-mexico-indigenous-and-federal-officials-urge-outcry-on-proposed-rollback-of-chaco-protections/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Q_DJJNUNJdfySNsasbK1Hx0Mqe-tg8meEXdaC7WmKbIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;BQWYiU3nVE14m8Vm7ro_7b-OVJoqVspS9hQGlTlZtn0ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;B7mrpyz_9vrnrdI8ysWAQfO5LBGXEuoC9bDknBoIKJoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx„https://ictnews.org/uncategorized/new-mexico-indigenous-and-federal-officials-urge-outcry-on-proposed-rollback-of-chaco-protections/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;XgdYo-dEOLQ-qSs5H3RZ2xOAJUZL3m7ALWIJL5HAu6Eƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyÉJoshua Bowling Source New Mexico Joey Sanchez, the chair of the All Pueblo Council of Governors, stood outside the U.S. Capitol Wednesday with dozens of tribal leaders and New Mexicoâ€™s entire federal delegation to protest a Bureau of Land Management proposal to unwind mining protections around Chaco Culture National Historical Park as just a week remains for the public to weigh in on the plan. The Bureau of Land Management recently opened a two-week period&nbsp; for the public to comment &nbsp;on&nbsp; a proposal to eliminate or shrink the 10-mile mining protections &nbsp;around Chaco, a spiritually and culturally significant site for many of New Mexicoâ€™s pueblos and tribes. Members of New Mexicoâ€™s federal delegation&nbsp; quickly denounced the plan &nbsp;when the comment period opened last week, and many noted that BLM and U.S. Department of the Interior officials had not visited with the Indigenous communities that the decision would impact. As a chorus of Indigenous leaders and politicians spoke at Wednesdayâ€™s event, they urged New Mexicans to submit a public comment on the BLM plan&nbsp; through its online portal . â€œChaco is not a museumâ€¦it is a living cultural landscape,â€ Sanchez said Wednesday. â€œThe pueblos have protected Chaco for thousands of years, and weâ€™re not going to stop today.â€ A view of the Pueblo Bonito at Chaco Canyon National Historical Park, which tribal leaders and advocates say is threatened by the Trump administrationâ€™s efforts to undo federal protections against oil and gas drilling. (Danielle Prokop/Source NM) U.S. Sen. Martin Heinrich, D-New Mexico, joined Sanchez in Washington, D.C., Wednesday and said that Chaco Canyon â€œis not for sale.â€ New Mexico has already positioned itself as&nbsp; a national leader in oil and gas production , and itâ€™s done so without sacrificing sacred sites, Heinrich, who serves as ranking member of the U.S. Senate Committee on Energy and Natural Resources, said. â€œWe produce far more electrons than we consume and we have never had to sacrifice our most sacred places to do that,â€ he said. â€œAnd we should not do that going forward.â€ Each of New Mexicoâ€™s U.S. senators and representatives, all Democrats, spoke out against the proposal at Wednesdayâ€™s event and said it was an â€œinsultâ€ that the federal government would allow just two weeks for public comment on a plan that could undo years of work to install Chacoâ€™s current protections. In 2025, they&nbsp; proposed legislation &nbsp;that would make those protections permanent. U.S. Rep. Teresa Leger FernÃ¡ndez, D-N.ew Mexico, spoke alongside New Mexico pueblo and tribal leaders in Washington, D.C., on July 22, 2026, to oppose a Bureau of Land Management plan to eliminate or reduce mining protections around Chaco Culture National Historical Park. (Screenshot) U.S. Rep. Teresa Leger FernÃ¡ndez, D-New Mexico, questioned whether itâ€™s necessary to drill any closer to Chaco than what is currently allowed. The BLMâ€™s current proposal lists three options: eliminate the 10-mile buffer zone; cut it in half; or keep it intact. Chaco sits in northwest New Mexico, near the Navajo Nation and the Four Corners region. About 1,000 years ago, it was the San Juan Basinâ€™s cultural and economic hub,&nbsp; according to the U.S. National Park Service . â€œA molecule of gas from Chaco is the same as a molecule of gas outside that 10-mile buffer zone,â€ Leger FernÃ¡ndez said. â€œThat molecule of gas could be easily, easily developed outside and without disturbing the 4,700â€¦archaeological sites within that 10 miles. If we let the bulldozers of the oil and gas companies destroy any of those 4,700 sites, they can never be reconstructed.â€ State leaders push back on BLM plan State lawmakers have also taken up the fight against the BLMâ€™s plan. Rep. Derrick J. Lente, D-Sandia Pueblo, on Tuesday called on Gov. Michelle Lujan Grisham and several state agencies to present a unified front against the federal plan. â€œChaco is not a bargaining chip, and it is not an ordinary parcel of federal land. It is the ancestral home of our people and a place of continuing ceremony and prayer,â€ Lente said in a statement. â€œAs a Pueblo person and as a legislator, I refuse to stay quiet while our tribal sovereignty is ignored and our sacred lands are opened to extraction.â€ In a statement to Source NM, a spokesperson for Lujan Grisham wrote that she is â€œfiercely opposedâ€ to the Trump administrationâ€™s proposal and noted that state Cultural Affairs Secretary Debra Garcia y Griego wrote a letter to BLM officials, urging them to keep the current protections in place. The governor â€œis working through appropriate channels to push back on this terribly shortsighted and reckless plan, including submitting official administration correspondence in opposition back in April and communicating with New Mexicoâ€™s congressional delegation,â€ Lujan Grishamâ€™s Communications Director Michael Coleman wrote in an email to Source NM. â€œShe encourages Rep. Lente, other members of the New Mexico Legislature and the general public to also submit letters in opposition to help protect Chaco Canyon.â€ The post New Mexico Indigenous and federal officials urge outcry on proposed rollback of Chaco protections appeared first on ICT .ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;JLQ_cyUFBpLByugAk4C61dBl19cUYFc-4avlonOvN48ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;RH-oFrQsVHKbXwmeGmq9vpFIRmiAre5pC8aDT1HyClsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;nMXyzV2qFjDm8krapIEE76DO_DkInEYvhqfPv_8Mmvcƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;EtZean1EmdWC7cjGC1C21qaG2_ZMr8Nz743NKLCdV9Iƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;aMmTZAlaaK3lqNCJOavwlzqqnzhuh8hzsTz5V9HB16wƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Dy4U4eWNitKvyFo6CXBv8cBM9Gk2mO2kBl2i7RnxTJoƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;apQN1a6QHsFEYNQm9UWrzu3UahxBK51LMog2s3NXGpcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒ