creenshot) U.S. Rep. Teresa Leger FernÃ¡ndez, D-New Mexico, questioned whether itâ€™s necessary to drill any closer to Chaco than what is currently allowed. The BLMâ€™s current proposal lists three options: eliminate the 10-mile buffer zone; cut it in half; or keep it intact. Chaco sits in northwest New Mexico, near the Navajo Nation and the Four Corners region. About 1,000 years ago, it was the San Juan Basinâ€™s cultural and economic hub,&nbsp; according to the U.S. National Park Service . â€œA molecule of gas from Chaco is the same as a molecule of gas outside that 10-mile buffer zone,â€ Leger FernÃ¡ndez said. â€œThat molecule of gas could be easily, easily developed outside and without disturbing the 4,700â€¦archaeological sites within that 10 miles. If we let the bulldozers of the oil and gas companies destroy any of those 4,700 sites, they can never be reconstructed.â€ State leaders push back on BLM plan State lawmakers have also taken up the fight against the BLMâ€™s plan. Rep. Derrick J. Lente, D-Sandia Pueblo, on Tuesday called on Gov. Michelle Lujan Grisham and several state agencies to present a unified front against the federal plan. â€œChaco is not a bargaining chip, and it is not an ordinary parcel of federal land. It is the ancestral home of our people and a place of continuing ceremony and prayer,â€ Lente said in a statement. â€œAs a Pueblo person and as a legislator, I refuse to stay quiet while our tribal sovereignty is ignored and our sacred lands are opened to extraction.â€ In a statement to Source NM, a spokesperson for Lujan Grisham wrote that she is â€œfiercely opposedâ€ to the Trump administrationâ€™s proposal and noted that state Cultural Affairs Secretary Debra Garcia y Griego wrote a letter to BLM officials, urging them to keep the current protections in place. The governor â€œis working through appropriate channels to push back on this terribly shortsighted and reckless plan, including submitting official administration correspondence in opposition back in April and communicating with New Mexicoâ€™s congressional delegation,â€ Lujan Grishamâ€™s Communications Director Michael Coleman wrote in an email to Source NM. â€œShe encourages Rep. Lente, other members of the New Mexico Legislature and the general public to also submit letters in opposition to help protect Chaco Canyon.â€ The post New Mexico Indigenous and federal officials urge outcry on proposed rollback of Chaco protections appeared first on ICT .ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;El-K-e_GhIVbAcZEhM_diW2AuVVcmfrHNh9dtpFOipwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxaNew Mexico Indigenous and federal officials urge outcry on proposed rollback of Chaco protectionsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;JEXqWKhSqPBa88ltUj0Ui3sg6CqllIEDkZV2pWuNlhMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkoSyndicationItemƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;GZdVtp2hGihyL5wAE-BvEetXXWlGqMiSG0efPpXkID8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx„https://ictnews.org/uncategorized/new-mexico-indigenous-and-federal-officials-urge-outcry-on-proposed-rollback-of-chaco-protections/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;tRufN58AwnSvHUCd-6XxLV0HvPSqYk2auI4SgbQOC2Uƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx`new-mexico-indigenous-and-federal-officials-urge-outcry-on-proposed-rollback-of-chaco-protectionƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2mHZk-bRiC_XQ8HnRi3pb6gJ5j3X4ZvCYalJV-UE7_8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkwSyndicationSourceBundleƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;o-u6xHBnwip8ZLwg2K555gBVnSQXquKnjy5QVAdkZXAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerigh